{Mossa 31 del bianco}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Ng3 e6 7. Bd3 c5 8. c3
(8. O-O {quite a bit more popular. Do we need the pawn to be on c3 $2}) 8... cxd4
9. Nxd4 Bc5 10. O-O Bxd4 {Flesch gives up the bishop pair to saddle Tal with an
isolated pawn. Committal and possibly questionable.} 11. cxd4 O-O 12. Bg5 h6 13.
Bf4 Nb6 14. Bc2 $5 {Already intending to sacrifice the rook on a1 $2} (14. Qd2 {The
direct idea to sacrifice on h6 \"almost\" wins.} 14... Nfd5 15. Bxh6 gxh6 16.
Qxh6 f5 $12 {leaves White with no better than a perpetual check.}) 14... Nbd5 15.
Be5 Qb6 16. Qd3 $5 {The move of a beginner or a Riga Magician.} 16... Nb4 17. Qd2
Nxc2 18. Bxf6 $3 {Of course, Tal is not going to just capture on c2 and be worse.
He sacrifices the rook on a1 and has just enough firepower remaining with the
queen and two minor pieces. Yes... he also has a rook, but it is bystanding for
now.} 18... Nxa1 (18... gxf6 $4 19. Qxh6 {intending Nh5 and checkmate.}) 19. Nh5 $1
(19. Rxa1 $2 Qb5 {defends.}) 19... e5 $1 {Flesch defends excellently. This was the
only try. Now the queen and bishop can defend.} (19... gxf6 20. Qxh6 $18) (19...
g5 20. Bxg5 $1 hxg5 21. Qxg5+ {mates.}) 20. dxe5 g5 (20... gxf6 21. Qxh6 {mates
again :D}) 21. e6 $3 {Brilliant $1 Black sacrificed the e6-pawn to allow the queen
AND bishop to defend. Tal gives it back to stuff the e6-square when only one of
those pieces can defend.} (21. Bxg5 $2 hxg5 $2 (21... Qg6 $1 {The queen must return to
the king's defense just in time. White will no longer checkmate.}) 22. Nf6+ $18)
(21. Rxa1 Bg4 $1 22. Ng3 {And Tal's attack lacks the steam to force victory.})
21... Qxe6 (21... Bxe6 {Maybe stronger $2} 22. Bxg5 Qc5 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Be3 Qc2
25. Qd4 {Tal still has a nice attack, but it is not clear if it is decisive.})
(21... fxe6 $2 22. Qd3 $18) 22. h3 $3 {Tal stops any ...Qg4 defenses and prepares g4
to defend the knight. He STILL hasn't captured the knight on a1, and despite
being down a rook, he takes time for a quite move that stabilizes his knight.}
(22. Bc3 f6 23. Rxa1 {Was also promising.}) 22... Qf5 23. Rxa1 {After regaining
the knight so that he is ONLY down a pawn, Tal's attack has slowed. However, he
is still much better. White's unopposed bishop and powerful knight are simply
better than the black rooks.} 23... Be6 24. Re1 Qg6 25. g4 Rac8 26. Bc3 Rfd8 27.
Qe3 Rd3 28. Qe5 Rcxc3 {The threat of mate on h8 forced Black to give back his
last trump - the extra exchange.} 29. bxc3 Kh7 $2 {permitting a winning knight
maneuver.} (29... Rd8 {There is still no forced win here although one suspects
that in the long term, there is an unforced one. Tal's pieces dominate.}) 30.
Nf6+ Kg7 31. Nd5+ Kh7 (31... Kf8 32. Qb8+ Kg7 33. Ne7 $18) 32. Ne7 {Flesch
resigned here as...} 32... Qg7 33. Qe4+ {wins the d3-rook.} 1-0


