{Mossa 36 del bianco (la partita è piena di mosse belle)}
1. e4 d6 {A surprise. Kasparov wrote that he had never faced this move in
classical games.} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Be3 Bg7 5. Qd2 c6 {Black must not
castle quickly in this line, otherwise White's attack becomes very dangerous. In
fact, it's still unclear what direction his king should take.} 6. f3 (6. Bh6
{immediately is also possible.}) 6... b5 7. Nge2 Nbd7 8. Bh6 Bxh6 9. Qxh6 Bb7
10. a3 {This was a novelty at the time.} 10... e5 (10... a5 $1 {is stronger, going
for quick counterplay with b5-b4.}) 11. O-O-O (11. dxe5 {is interesting:} 11...
dxe5 (11... Nxe5 $6 {is a positional mistake, since White gets a more pleasant
pawn structure after} 12. Nd4) 12. Nc1 Nc5 13. Nd3 Nxd3+ 14. Bxd3 Qe7 15. O-O
Ng8 16. Qe3 Nf6 {White has a small advantage and the character of the game is
calmer.}) 11... Qe7 (11... a5 {is again possible. White had the advantage after}
12. Kb1 b4 13. Na4 {in Ma-Bachmann, Barcelona 2016.}) 12. Kb1 a6 (12... a5 {is
again an interesting plan, but White still has the upper-hand after} 13. Nc1 b4
14. dxe5 Nxe5 $1 15. Na4 bxa3 16. b3 O-O-O 17. Qe3) 13. Nc1 (13. dxe5 dxe5 (13...
Nxe5 14. Nd4 {with the advantage.}) 14. Nc1 O-O-O 15. Nb3 Nc5 {transposes to
later analysis.}) (13. g4 {was suggested by Kasparov.}) 13... O-O-O (13... exd4 $1
{is better, preventing the capture on e5.} 14. Rxd4 O-O-O) 14. Nb3 {L'Ami points
out that this move is inaccurate.} (14. dxe5 $1 {leads to an advantage for White:}
14... dxe5 15. Nb3 Nc5 16. Nxc5 Qxc5 (16... Rxd1+ $2 {has a tactical flaw:} 17.
Nxd1 Qxc5 18. Bxb5 $1 {The idea of this creative move is to keep the d1-knight
defended.} (18. Qg7 $2 Rd8 {attacking the knight on d1.}) 18... cxb5 19. Qg7
{winning a pawn.}) 17. Be2 Qe7 18. Na2 $1 {A subtle positional maneuver, improving
the knight's position.} 18... Nd7 19. Nc1 {with advantage for White. The knight
can go to b3 and then a5. If Black advances c6-c5, then White can play c2-c4.
The position is more unpleasant than it seems at first glance.}) 14... exd4 $1 15.
Rxd4 c5 16. Rd1 Nb6 $1 {Black prepares the advance d6-d5.} 17. g3 (17. Qe3
{prevents d5, but Black still manages to equalize:} 17... Nfd5 $1 (17... Rhe8 18.
Qf2) 18. Nxd5 Bxd5) 17... Kb8 (17... d5 {Topalov could have advanced the pawn
immediately.} 18. Qf4 d4 {Forced.} 19. Bh3+ Nfd7 {L'Ami finishes his analysis
here. Let's go further:} 20. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 (20... Nxd5 {is less accurate:} 21. exd5
Bxd5 $2 (21... g5 {is better.}) 22. Rhe1 Be6 23. Na5 {with a winning attack.}) 21.
exd5 Nxd5 22. Qe4 Qxe4 (22... Qd6 $2 {loses to} 23. Nxd4 $1 cxd4 24. Rxd4) 23. fxe4
Ne3 24. Nxc5 f5 $1 25. Ne6 $1 {Amazing play.} 25... Rde8 26. exf5 gxf5 27. Nxd4 Nxd1
28. Rxd1 Ne5 29. Bxf5+ Kb8 {White has two pawns for the exchange and the endgame
is balanced.}) 18. Na5 Ba8 {The most natural move, but 18...d5 is also
possible.} (18... d5 19. Nxb7 Kxb7 20. exd5 Nfxd5 21. Nxd5 Rxd5 22. Bd3 f5 {with
equality.}) 19. Bh3 d5 20. Qf4+ Ka7 21. Rhe1 d4 (21... dxe4 $2 {is a big mistake.
After} 22. fxe4 {the opening of the f-file and ideas with Nc3-d5 give White a
clear advantage.}) 22. Nd5 {Kasparov attaches a dubious sign to this move, but
it's actually the best.} (22. Na2 {is too passive. I'd never imagine Kasparov
playing this.}) 22... Nbxd5 23. exd5 Qd6 24. Rxd4 $1 {It's important to note that
Kasparov had to see this move and its consequences before placing the knight on
d5, since otherwise the white position is practically losing.} 24... cxd4 $2 {This
move is a mistake, but we need to recognize Topalov's confidence and courage.
According to Kasparov's account, Topalov believed that this move would lead to a
forced draw and that the sequence would be entertaining for spectators.} (24...
Bxd5 {leads to a draw:} 25. Rxd5 $1 Nxd5 26. Qxf7+ Nc7 27. Re6 Qd1+ 28. Ka2 Rd7
29. Re7 (29. Nc6+ {is perpetual check after} 29... Kb7 $8 30. Na5+ Ka7) 29...
Qd5+ $1 30. Qxd5 Rxd5 31. Rxc7+ Kb6 32. Rc6+ Kxa5 33. Bc8 $1 Rxc8 34. Rxc8 c4 {Black
has enough counterplay to make a draw.}) (24... Rhe8 $5 {L'Ami mentions this move
as a suggestion by modern engines. I need to take advantage of this opportunity
to enter unknown territory in this famous game, as I found a very interesting
sequence after this move.} 25. Rxe8 Nxe8 26. Qxf7+ Kb6 27. Nc6 $3 {The only move.
Mate on a7 is threatened, so the knight has to be captured.} 27... Bxc6 (27...
cxd4 28. Qa7#) 28. dxc6 Qxd4 {The right capture.} (28... cxd4 $2 {loses:} 29. Qb7+
Kc5 (29... Ka5 30. b4+ Ka4 31. Qxa6#) 30. b4+ Kd5 (30... Kc4 31. Qf7+ Qd5 32.
Be6 {wins.}) 31. c7+ {with an easy win for White.}) 29. Qb7+ Ka5 30. c7 {The
most dangerous.} (30. Bc8 {is a draw after} 30... Rxc8 31. Qxc8 Qd1+ 32. Ka2
Qxc2 33. Qxe8 Qc4+) 30... Qg1+ $1 31. Ka2 Rd1 $1 {The only way. Black's counterplay
is in time.} 32. b4+ (32. c8=Q $2 Ra1+ 33. Kb3 Qe3+ 34. c3 c4+ 35. Kc2 Qe2#) (32.
Qxa6+ Kxa6 33. c8=Q+ {should end in a draw.}) 32... cxb4 33. axb4+ Kxb4 34. Qe4+
Ka5 35. c8=Q Ra1+ 36. Kb3 Rb1+ 37. Ka2 $1 (37. Ka3 $2 Qc1+ 38. Ka2 Qb2#) (37. Kc3 $2
b4+ 38. Kc4 Nd6+ {It's a bit unusual to fork two of your opponent's queens $1})
37... Ra1+ {with perpetual check. As mentioned at the beginning of the article,
with each new analysis of a classical game it's possible to discover new
treasures. This variation after 24...Rhe8 is one of them.}) (24... Rhf8 $5 {Even
this is possible $1 The idea is to protect the pawn on f7. I haven't seen this
move analyzed or suggested before.} 25. c4 $5 {Of course this is the computer's
suggestion.} (25. Qxd6 Rxd6 26. Rdd1 Rxd5 (26... Kb6 27. Nc6 Bxc6 28. dxc6 Rxd1+
29. Rxd1 Kxc6 {with an equal endgame.}) 27. c4 $1 Rxd1+ 28. Rxd1 {with equality.})
25... cxd4 (25... bxc4 {is possible, but here White can fight for a small plus
after the queen exchange.}) 26. Qxd4+ (26. Re7+ Kb8 27. Qxd4 {transposes to the
main line.}) 26... Kb8 27. Re7 (27. c5 {is also interesting:} 27... Qc7 28. Qxf6
Rxd5 (28... Qxa5 29. Qf4+ Qc7 30. Qxc7+ Kxc7 31. Re7+ Kb8 32. c6 f5 $1 {A
miraculous defense.} 33. c7+ Kc8 34. cxd8=Q+ Kxd8 $1 {The endgame looks dangerous
for Black, but the computer claims equality.} (34... Rxd8 35. g4 $1 {Some
pressure.})) (28... Rfe8 29. Rxe8 Rxe8 30. b4 $1 Bxd5 31. Qxa6 Qe5 $1 {and Black
maintains equality.}) 29. b4 Qd8 $1 {The only move.} 30. Qxa6 Rd1+ 31. Rxd1 Qxd1+
32. Kb2 Qd2+ {with perpetual check.})) (24... Kb6 $1 {is the best move. Here White
should be careful and fight for equality.} 25. b4 $1 (25. Nb3 $6 Bxd5 26. Qxd6+
Rxd6 {with a better endgame for Black.}) 25... Qxf4 26. Rxf4 Nxd5 27. Rxf7 cxb4
28. axb4 Nxb4 29. Nb3 Rd6 30. Re6 $1 {with equal chances.}) 25. Re7+ $1 {The rook
cannot be captured and Black's king begins its journey.} (25. Qxd4+ $2 {loses
after} 25... Qb6 26. Re7+ Nd7) 25... Kb6 (25... Qxe7 $2 26. Qxd4+ Kb8 27. Qb6+ Bb7
28. Nc6+ Ka8 29. Qa7#) (25... Kb8 {also loses:} 26. Qxd4 Nd7 27. Bxd7 Bxd5 28.
c4 $1 {An accurate move to close the bishop's diagonal.} (28. Nc6+ $2 Qxc6 $1 29. Bxc6
Ba2+ 30. Kxa2 Rxd4) 28... Qxe7 (28... bxc4 29. Nc6+ $1 {Now this works.}) 29. Qb6+
Ka8 30. Qxa6+ Kb8 31. Qb6+ Ka8 32. Bc6+ Bxc6 33. Nxc6 {with a win.}) 26. Qxd4+
Kxa5 (26... Qc5 {has a pretty refutation:} 27. Qxf6+ Qd6 {During the course of
the game and the analysis, the reader will notice that there are many moments in
which brilliant moves are executed—a true chess feast. This is another one of
them.} 28. Be6 $3 {An exotic and deadly move. The bishop puts itself in an
eccentric position and the knight on a5 remains ignored, but calculation
justifies everything.} 28... Bxd5 (28... fxe6 29. Rxe6 {winning the queen.})
(28... Rhe8 29. b4 $1 {Finally time to defend the brave knight.} 29... Rxe7 30.
Qd4+ Kc7 31. Qa7+ Bb7 32. Qxb7#) (28... Kxa5 29. b4+ Ka4 (29... Kb6 30. Qd4+)
30. Qc3 $1 Bxd5 31. Kb2 $1 Bxe6 32. Qb3+ Bxb3 33. cxb3# {This delicious mating theme
will appear many times.}) 29. b4 $1 Bc6 (29... Bxe6 30. Rb7#) 30. Qxf7 Qd1+ 31.
Kb2 Qxf3 32. Qg7 {with a winning attack for White.}) 27. b4+ Ka4 28. Qc3 $6 {For
a long time it was believed that this move was a mistake, but L'Ami and the
powerful computers demonstrated that it also wins. In any case, it must be
admitted that there is a more forceful way to win.} (28. Re6 $6 {is a creative
attempt:} 28... fxe6 29. Qe3 $1 {threatening mate on b3.} 29... Qxb4+ $1 (29...
Bxd5 $2 30. Kb2 {followed by Qb3.}) 30. axb4 Kxb4 31. Qd4+ Ka5 32. d6 $1 (32. Qxf6 $6
Bxd5 {with equality.}) 32... Nd5 33. Bxe6 Rhe8 $1 34. Bf7 $1 (34. Bxd5 Rxd6 {is
equal.}) 34... Rf8 $1 35. Bxd5 Bxd5 36. Qxd5 Kb6 37. Qd4+ Kc6 38. Qg7 $1 {White has
good winning chances.}) (28. Ra7 $3 {As far as I know this move was found by the
late GM Ljubomir Kavalek. It's amazing that White can play such a calm move.
However, with the pressure on a7 Black's queen is now limited and White gains
additional chances.} 28... Bb7 (28... Nxd5 29. Rxa6+ $1 Qxa6 30. Qb2 $1 Nc3+ 31.
Qxc3 Bd5 32. Kb2 {with the typical Qb3 and pawn mate.}) (28... Bxd5 29. Qc3 $1
Rhe8 30. Kb2 Re2 {The mate on b3 is prevented and now White switches gears.} 31.
Qc7 $1 Qxc7 32. Rxa6+ Qa5 33. Rxa5#) 29. Rxb7 Qxd5 (29... Nxd5 30. Bd7 $3 {Another
amazing shot. At the same time that White threatens to take the b5-pawn, he
prevents the knight from moving.} (30. Qb2 $2 Nxb4 {and Black wins.}) 30... Rxd7
31. Qb2 $1 Nxb4 32. Rxd7 Qc5 33. Rd4 Rc8 34. Qb3+ Ka5 35. axb4+ {winning the
queen.}) 30. Rb6 $1 a5 (30... Qxd4 31. Rxa6#) (30... Ra8 31. Qxf6 a5 32. Bf1 $1
{with a decisive attack.}) 31. Ra6 $1 Ra8 32. Qe3 $3 {A fantastic move and the only
way to win.} (32. Qc3 $2 {is not enough due to} 32... Qc4 $1) 32... Rxa6 33. Kb2
{Threatening Qb3 again.} 33... axb4 34. axb4 {The mating theme on b3 is
definitely over, but even so, Black doesn't escape from his fate.} 34... Qa2+
(34... Kxb4 35. Qc3+ Ka4 36. Qa3#) 35. Kxa2 Kxb4+ 36. Kb2 {The beauty of these
variations brings tears to my eyes to this day. The conclusion is that 28.Ra7
wins by attack and categorically, but in several of the lines White has to find
a very difficult and forced move.}) 28... Qxd5 (28... Bxd5 $2 29. Kb2 {with
unavoidable mate.}) 29. Ra7 $1 {This was missed by Topalov, but who can blame
him $2} (29. Qc7 $2 Qd1+ 30. Kb2 Qd4+ {Apparently Topalov had calculated this
drawing line when accepting the sacrifice.}) 29... Bb7 (29... Rd6 {loses to} 30.
Kb2) 30. Rxb7 {White takes his time to capture the bishop. He is a rook down and
has no threats for the next move, but still Black is unable to save the game.}
30... Qc4 $1 {The best defense.} (30... Rd6 31. Rb6 $3 (31. Kb2 $2 Qd4) 31... Rxb6
32. Kb2 {with the typical Qb3 next.}) (30... Ne4 31. fxe4 Qc4 32. Ra7 $1 Rd1+
(32... Ra8 33. Qe3 Rxa7 34. Kb2 {with mate.}) 33. Kb2 Qxc3+ 34. Kxc3 Rd6 35. e5
Rb6 36. Kb2 {The endgame is winning for White. His plan is to bring the bishop
into the attack.}) (30... Rhe8 31. Rb6 Ra8 32. Bf1 $3 {The only winning move. The
c4-square is taken from the queen. White threatens 33.Rd6.} 32... Rec8 (32...
Nd7 33. Rd6 $1) (32... Re1+ 33. Qxe1 Nd7 34. Rb7 $3 {Again the only move.} 34...
Qxb7 (34... Ne5 35. Qc3 Qxf3 36. Bd3 {with Qb3 mate coming.}) 35. Qd1 $1 {Another
precious move.} (35. Qc3 $2 {doesn't work:} 35... Qd5 36. Kb2 Qe5) 35... Kxa3 36.
c3 {followed by 37.Qc1 and mate.}) 33. Qxc8 Qd1+ 34. Ka2 Qd5+ 35. Bc4 Qxc4+ 36.
Qxc4 bxc4 37. Rxf6 $18) 31. Qxf6 Kxa3 $6 (31... Rd1+ $1 {is a better chance, but not
enough. This is an important moment, since Kasparov's original analysis has been
improved.} 32. Kb2 Ra8 (32... a5 $2 33. Ra7 $18) 33. Qb6 Qd4+ 34. Qxd4 Rxd4 35.
Bd7 $3 {A wonderful prophylactic move to stop a6-a5 and to prepare Bc6, chasing
the rook on a8. This move was missed by Kasparov and pointed out by L'Ami. The
variations are really hard to understand, but I beg the reader to take the time.
It will be worth it.} (35. Rxf7 $2 a5 $1 36. Be6 axb4 37. Bb3+ Ka5 38. axb4+ Kb6
(38... Rxb4 $2 39. c3) 39. Rxh7 Rf8 $1 {with good drawing chances.}) 35... Rd6 {This
is necessary to avoid Bc6.} 36. g4 $3 {Again the only winning move $1 After some
time I finally understood White's idea. Black is paralyzed and the secret of the
position is being able to maneuver the light-squared bishop to deliver mate. The
a8-rook can never move because White captures on b5 and then mates on a7. For
this reason, White's rook needs to be on b7. It turns out that the only possible
plan for Black is to keep the rook on the sixth rank, limiting the bishop's
movement. What White needs to do is force the rook to leave the sixth rank, and
to do this it's necessary to advance the pawns to restrict the rook's movement.}
(36. f4 $2 f5 37. h3 h5 38. h4 Rf6 {With equality (L'Ami).}) 36... Rf6 (36... h6
37. h4 $1 h5 38. gxh5 gxh5 39. Bf5 {The bishop comes to fulfil the final mission.}
39... Rd2 40. Be4 a5 41. Rc7 Ra6 42. Bb7 Rad6 (42... Ra7 43. Bd5 $1 Rxc7 44. Bb3#)
43. Bc6 {with a win.}) (36... g5 37. Bf5 a5 38. c3 Rd2+ 39. Bc2+ Rxc2+ 40. Kxc2
{with an easily winning rook endgame.}) 37. f4 $3 {Forced and good.} 37... Rxf4
(37... Rd6 38. g5 {Black is tied up.}) 38. Bc6 $1 Rc4 (38... Rd4 39. Rxf7 Rc8 40.
Rf6 a5 41. Bf3 $1 {The rook goes to a6 and White wins.}) 39. Bd5 Rxc2+ 40. Kxc2
Kxa3 41. Rxb5 {with a winning endgame.}) 32. Qxa6+ Kxb4 33. c3+ $1 {This subtle
check with the pawn disorganizes the black pieces.} 33... Kxc3 34. Qa1+ Kd2
(34... Kb4 35. Qb2+ $1 Ka5 (35... Qb3 36. Rxb5+) 36. Qa3+ Qa4 37. Ra7+ {winning
the queen.}) 35. Qb2+ Kd1 (35... Ke1 36. Re7+ $1) 36. Bf1 $3 {Another gem.} 36...
Rd2 (36... Qxf1 37. Qc2+ Ke1 38. Re7+ {ends in mate.}) 37. Rd7 $1 {I remember
Kasparov commenting—if I'm not mistaken this happened while he was demonstrating
the game to the journalists—that White can only win in this position because the
black pawn is on g6 $1} 37... Rxd7 38. Bxc4 bxc4 39. Qxh8 {That's it.} 39... Rd3
40. Qa8 c3 41. Qa4+ Ke1 42. f4 f5 43. Kc1 Rd2 44. Qa7 {Simply unforgettable.}
1-0


